-### Parameters and Typical Values
-*   **Tissue Values ($f_{vn}$):** Specific to the dataset (e.g., CT numbers for air, fat, muscle, bone).
-*   **Target Opacities ($\alpha_{vn}$):** Typically low for interiors, high for boundaries. The gradient scaling handles the boundary enhancement, so $\alpha_{vn}$ can be uniform or varied.
-*   **Samples per Ray ($K$):** Depends on resolution and view. Sources cite $K$ as a variable in complexity analysis [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Super-sampling:** Optional. Interpolating additional samples between acquired ones reduces aliasing but increases cost [S1, S2, unofficial].
+## Subtopics
 
-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Adjacency Violation:** If non-adjacent tissue types touch, voxels at boundaries cannot be unambiguously classified, leading to incorrect rendering [S1, S2, unofficial]. Solution: Interactive clipping or segmentation [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Thin Features:** Binary thresholds may miss thin features. This method renders them as faint wisps [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Gradient Artifacts:** CT scanner anisotropy and patient motion cause striping because shading depends on gradient orientation [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Rotation Artifacts:** Fixing colors/opacities while rotating causes light sources to appear to travel with the data [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Transition Region Thickness:** The thickness of the transition region around isosurfaces remains constant only if the local gradient magnitude is constant within a radius of $r$ voxels [S1, S2, unofficial].
+### [Piecewise Density Approximation for Reparameterized Sampling](1139-piecewise-density-approximation-for-reparameterized-sampling.md)
+Uses piecewise functions for density but focuses on reparameterization for sampling efficiency rather than gradient-based surface enhancement. Choose when sampling efficiency is the primary bottleneck.
+
+### [Opacity Correction for Slice Count Invariance](1277-opacity-correction-for-slice-count-invariance.md)
+Adjusts opacity to maintain visual consistency regardless of slice count. Choose when varying resolution or slice spacing is a concern.
+
+### [Direct Volume Rendering with Interactive Piecewise Transfer Function](2068-direct-volume-rendering-with-interactive-piecewise-transfer-.md)
+General piecewise transfer function definition without the specific gradient scaling and adjacency constraints. Choose when tissue adjacency is not guaranteed or more complex opacity ramps are needed.
+
+### [Alpha-to-Weight Computation (Forward)](2373-alpha-to-weight-computation-forward.md)
+Focuses on the compositing weight calculation. Choose when the transfer function is already defined and compositing accuracy is the issue.
+
+### [SDF-based Opacity Derivation via Logistic CDF](3233-sdf-based-opacity-derivation-via-logistic-cdf.md)
+Uses Signed Distance Fields and logistic functions for opacity. Choose when smooth, distance-based opacity transitions are preferred over piecewise linear tissue mappings.
+
+### [Direct Volume Rendering with Phong Shading](3686-direct-volume-rendering-with-phong-shading.md)
+Adds Phong shading to volume rendering. Choose when surface lighting effects are desired in addition to opacity mapping.
+
+### [Linear Opacity Interpolation](7016-linear-opacity-interpolation.md)
+Simple linear interpolation of opacity without gradient scaling. Choose when surface enhancement is not required.
+
+### [Programmable Transfer Function DVR](8220-programmable-transfer-function-dvr.md)
+Uses GPU shaders for transfer functions. Choose for flexible, real-time transfer function editing.
+
+### [Silhouette Shading](8222-silhouette-shading.md)
